CAT = "cparegProperty"; SEG = "PRP"
Q = [
("A business buys a machine for $40,000 and pays $2,400 of sales tax, $1,100 of freight and $1,500 to install it. The machine's tax basis is:",
 "$45,000", ["$40,000", "$42,400", "$43,500"],
 "Basis includes the price and every cost to acquire the asset and get it ready for use: $40,000 + $2,400 + $1,100 + $1,500 = $45,000."),
("Lee converts her personal car, which cost $30,000 and is now worth $18,000, to full business use. Her basis for depreciation is:",
 "$18,000", ["$30,000", "$24,000", "$12,000"],
 "Property converted from personal to business use is depreciated from the lower of its adjusted basis or its fair market value at conversion, here $18,000."),
("Kim receives stock as a gift. The donor's basis was $8,000 and its value at the gift was $5,000; no gift tax was paid. Kim later sells it for $9,500. Her gain is:",
 "$1,500", ["$4,500", "$0", "$9,500"],
 "For a sale at a gain, a donee uses the donor's basis of $8,000. $9,500 - $8,000 = $1,500."),
("Omar receives land as a gift: donor's basis $12,000, value at the gift $7,000, no gift tax. He sells it for $6,200. His recognized loss is:",
 "$800", ["$5,800", "$0", "$5,000"],
 "When value at the gift is below the donor's basis, the basis for a loss is that lower value, $7,000. $6,200 - $7,000 = an $800 loss."),
("Pat receives a gift of shares: donor's basis $20,000, value at the gift $15,000. Pat sells them for $17,000. Pat's result is:",
 "No gain or loss", ["A $3,000 loss", "A $2,000 gain", "A $5,000 loss"],
 "Using the donor's basis gives no gain, and using the $15,000 value gives no loss. A sale between the two values produces neither gain nor loss."),
("Raj inherits land the decedent bought for $50,000. It was worth $120,000 at death, and no alternate valuation was elected. Raj sells it 4 months later for $125,000. His gain is:",
 "$5,000 long-term", ["$5,000 short-term", "$75,000 long-term", "$75,000 short-term"],
 "Inherited property takes a basis of its value at death, $120,000, and is always treated as held long term. $125,000 - $120,000 = $5,000."),
("Tom sells 100 shares at a $1,200 loss and buys 100 shares of the same stock 20 days later for $5,000. His basis in the new shares is:",
 "$6,200", ["$5,000", "$3,800", "$6,800"],
 "The wash sale rule disallows the loss and adds it to the basis of the replacement shares: $5,000 + $1,200 = $6,200."),
("A new business incurs $53,000 of start-up costs and begins operating on July 1. Its maximum deduction for start-up costs in the first year is:",
 "$3,700", ["$2,000", "$5,000", "$3,767"],
 "The $5,000 immediate deduction is cut by the $3,000 over $50,000, leaving $2,000. The other $51,000 is amortized over 180 months: $283.33 x 6 months = $1,700. Total $3,700."),
("A new corporation incurs $4,000 of organizational costs. With the deemed election, its first-year deduction for them is:",
 "The full $4,000", ["Only 1/180 per month", "Nothing until it dissolves", "Half, the rest later"],
 "Up to $5,000 of organizational costs may be deducted in the first year, reduced only when total costs exceed $50,000. All $4,000 is deductible."),
("Points and fees paid to obtain a 10-year business loan are recovered by:",
 "Amortizing over the loan term", ["Deducting all in year one", "Adding them to asset basis", "Amortizing over 15 years"],
 "Costs of getting business debt are amortized over the life of the loan. Any unamortized balance is deducted if the loan is paid off early."),
("A buyer acquires a customer list and goodwill as part of buying a business. These intangibles are amortized over:",
 "15 years", ["5 years", "40 years", "Their useful lives"],
 "Section 197 intangibles acquired in a business purchase, including goodwill and customer lists, are amortized straight-line over 15 years (180 months)."),
("On April 1 a calendar-year buyer pays $180,000 for goodwill in a business acquisition. Its amortization this year is:",
 "$9,000", ["$12,000", "$6,000", "$10,000"],
 "Section 197 amortization starts in the month of acquisition: $180,000 / 180 months = $1,000 a month, times 9 months (April to December) = $9,000."),
("Under MACRS, a residential rental building is depreciated using:",
 "27.5 years, mid-month", ["39 years, mid-month", "27.5 years, half-year", "40 years, mid-quarter"],
 "Residential rental property is recovered straight-line over 27.5 years with the mid-month convention. Nonresidential real property uses 39 years."),
("A business places a newly bought office building in service. Its MACRS recovery period is:",
 "39 years", ["27.5 years", "31.5 years", "40 years"],
 "Nonresidential real property placed in service now is recovered over 39 years. 31.5 years applied only to buildings placed in service before May 1993."),
("A business buys a property for $900,000: $200,000 for the land and $700,000 for the building. Its depreciable basis is:",
 "$700,000", ["$900,000", "$200,000", "$450,000"],
 "Land is never depreciated, so only the $700,000 allocated to the building is depreciable."),
("The mid-quarter convention is required when more than what share of a year's depreciable personal property is placed in service in the last quarter?",
 "40%", ["25%", "50%", "30%"],
 "If more than 40% of the depreciable basis of personal property placed in service during the year falls in the last 3 months, the mid-quarter convention applies to all of it."),
("A business buys 7-year MACRS equipment for $50,000 and claims no Section 179 or bonus depreciation. Using 200% declining balance and the half-year convention, first-year depreciation is:",
 "$7,145", ["$14,286", "$3,571", "$5,355"],
 "The 200% rate on a 7-year life is 28.57%, halved for the half-year convention to 14.29%. $50,000 x 14.29% = $7,145."),
("When a business uses Section 179, bonus depreciation and regular MACRS on one asset, they are applied in which order?",
 "179, then bonus, then MACRS", ["Bonus, then 179, then MACRS", "MACRS, then 179, then bonus", "179, then MACRS, then bonus"],
 "Section 179 expensing comes first, bonus depreciation applies to the remaining basis, and regular MACRS depreciates whatever basis is left."),
("A Section 179 deduction that exceeds the business income limit for the year is:",
 "Carried forward", ["Lost permanently", "Carried back 2 years", "Converted to bonus"],
 "Section 179 cannot exceed taxable income from the active conduct of a trade or business. The excess carries forward to later years."),
("Which asset is NOT eligible for a Section 179 deduction?",
 "An apartment building", ["Office furniture", "A delivery van", "Off-the-shelf software"],
 "Section 179 covers tangible personal property, off-the-shelf software and certain improvements to nonresidential buildings. Residential rental buildings do not qualify."),
]
